1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,350
The Curiosity Club.

2
00:00:02,350 --> 00:00:04,646
Episode 17.

3
00:00:04,646 --> 00:00:06,970
Who Took a Bite of the Moon?

4
00:00:06,970 --> 00:00:09,687
Here is something to listen for.

5
00:00:09,687 --> 00:00:13,994
Does the Moon actually lose a piece when it looks like a thin smile?

6
00:00:13,994 --> 00:00:15,421
Ready?

7
00:00:15,421 --> 00:00:17,500
Let us find out.

8
00:00:17,500 --> 00:00:20,190
Maya draws the Moon on Monday.

9
00:00:20,190 --> 00:00:22,744
It looks like a thin smile.

10
00:00:22,744 --> 00:00:26,399
Several evenings later, she draws it again.

11
00:00:26,399 --> 00:00:29,836
This time, the bright part looks larger.

12
00:00:29,836 --> 00:00:31,915
Who is changing it?

13
00:00:31,915 --> 00:00:33,858
Leo asks.

14
00:00:33,858 --> 00:00:38,192
Perhaps someone keeps taking bites and then putting them back.

15
00:00:38,192 --> 00:00:41,154
Pip searches his imaginary menu.

16
00:00:41,154 --> 00:00:44,266
Moon pie: one enormous serving.

17
00:00:44,266 --> 00:00:48,654
Ms. Park brings a ball and a lamp into a darkened classroom.

18
00:00:48,654 --> 00:00:53,152
The lamp represents the Sun, and the ball represents the Moon.

19
00:00:53,152 --> 00:00:57,038
Maya stands in one place to be the observer on Earth.

20
00:00:57,038 --> 00:00:59,619
The lamp lights half the ball.

21
00:00:59,619 --> 00:01:05,611
As Ms. Park moves the ball around Maya, Maya sees different amounts of the bright half.

22
00:01:05,611 --> 00:01:09,089
The whole ball is still there, Maya says.

23
00:01:09,089 --> 00:01:12,595
I just cannot see all of its lit side from here.

24
00:01:12,595 --> 00:01:15,516
Exactly, says Ms. Park.

25
00:01:15,516 --> 00:01:18,097
The Moon reflects sunlight.

26
00:01:18,097 --> 00:01:22,635
Its changing position gives us different views of its sunlit half.

27
00:01:22,635 --> 00:01:26,970
Leo asks whether Earth's shadow makes the ordinary phases.

28
00:01:26,970 --> 00:01:33,532
Ms. Park explains that Earth's shadow causes a lunar eclipse when the objects line up appropriately.

29
00:01:33,532 --> 00:01:35,910
That is a different event.

30
00:01:35,910 --> 00:01:39,728
Pip writes a notice: The Moon has not been eaten.

31
00:01:39,728 --> 00:01:43,111
Please cancel the emergency bakery meeting.

32
00:01:43,111 --> 00:01:46,222
That evening, Maya looks up again.

33
00:01:46,222 --> 00:01:52,309
She draws both the bright shape she can see and a faint circle to remind herself of the whole Moon.

34
00:01:52,309 --> 00:01:55,271
It is still round, she says.

35
00:01:55,271 --> 00:01:57,826
My view is what changes.

36
00:01:57,826 --> 00:02:00,733
Now, a little more to discover.

37
00:02:00,733 --> 00:02:04,687
The Moon does not make its own visible light like the Sun.

38
00:02:04,687 --> 00:02:08,260
We see sunlight reflected from its surface.

39
00:02:08,260 --> 00:02:13,288
As it orbits Earth, we see different portions of its illuminated half.

40
00:02:13,288 --> 00:02:16,073
These views are called phases.

41
00:02:16,073 --> 00:02:19,986
Ordinary Moon phases are not caused by Earth's shadow.

42
00:02:19,986 --> 00:02:22,826
Let us hear the main idea again.

43
00:02:22,826 --> 00:02:27,459
The ball stayed whole while Maya saw different amounts of its lit half.

44
00:02:27,459 --> 00:02:31,100
This helped explain the Moon's changing appearance.

45
00:02:31,100 --> 00:02:37,635
Earth's shadow belongs in the explanation of a lunar eclipse, not the ordinary monthly phases.

46
00:02:37,635 --> 00:02:40,611
Here are three useful expressions.

47
00:02:40,611 --> 00:02:43,736
You can listen, or say them with me.

48
00:02:43,736 --> 00:02:46,073
It looks different tonight.

49
00:02:46,073 --> 00:02:48,410
It looks different tonight.

50
00:02:48,410 --> 00:02:51,073
The whole object is still there.

51
00:02:51,073 --> 00:02:53,736
The whole object is still there.

52
00:02:53,736 --> 00:02:56,480
What changes from our point of view?

53
00:02:56,480 --> 00:02:59,225
What changes from our point of view?

54
00:02:59,225 --> 00:03:01,820
Remember our listening question?

55
00:03:01,820 --> 00:03:06,127
Does the Moon actually lose a piece when it looks like a thin smile?

56
00:03:06,127 --> 00:03:08,260
Here is the answer.

57
00:03:08,260 --> 00:03:09,741
No.

58
00:03:09,741 --> 00:03:14,687
We are seeing only a small part of its sunlit half from our position on Earth.

59
00:03:14,687 --> 00:03:18,342
And here is a question just for your imagination.

60
00:03:18,342 --> 00:03:21,847
How could you keep a simple Moon diary for a week?

61
00:03:21,847 --> 00:03:24,959
There is more than one possible answer.

62
00:03:24,959 --> 00:03:28,206
Keep wondering, and see you next time.
